/-! # Vanishing in the universal curve test

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0EYU, second proof, final universal curve-test argument;
blueprint sec-smooth-base-change, sk-thm-vanishing
(blueprint: sbc-thm-universal-test-vanishing).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Etale

/-- Positive higher direct images of the constant cyclic abelian sheaf from the geometric
 generic fibre of a smooth affine relative curve vanish. The signed integer coefficient is
 represented by `ZMod d.natAbs`, since the ideals generated by `d` and `|d|` coincide.

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0EYU, second proof, final universal curve-test argument
(blueprint: sbc-thm-universal-test-vanishing).

Proof plan:
1. Put $n=|d|$. Since $A$ is a nonzero domain and $d$ is a unit, $n\geq1$; the ideals $(d)$ and
$(n)$ in $\mathbf Z$ coincide and $n$ remains a unit after every restriction and field extension
below. Thus we work with $M=\mathbf Z/n\mathbf Z$ without excluding negative integers $d$. By
[sbc-lem-higher-direct-image-presheaf], $R^qh_*\underline M$ is associated with
$P_q(U)=H^q(U\times_XY,\underline M)=H^q(U\times_S\operatorname{Spec}K,\underline M)$. The
equality of fibre products follows by composing the two Cartesian squares. It suffices to kill
every such class after an etale cover; the elementary sheafification argument is given in the last
step. Affine opens cover each etale object, so it suffices to start with affine $U$.

2. For affine etale $U\to X$, the composite $U\to S$ is smooth of relative dimension $0+1=1$ by
[lem-sbc-smooth-relative-dimension-permanence]; its fibre $C=U\times_S\operatorname{Spec}K$ is
smooth of relative dimension one by the same node. It is affine: if $U=\operatorname{Spec}B$, then
$C=\operatorname{Spec}(B\otimes_AK)$. Smoothness gives local finite presentation; affineness makes
$C\to\operatorname{Spec}K$ quasi-compact and separated, hence finite type. To allow disconnected
or empty $C$, use [lem-sc-smooth-curve-connected-coproduct] to decompose it into finitely many
connected smooth curves $C_j$. Their inclusions are open and closed; being closed in affine $C$,
each $C_j$ is affine. Apply [sbc-thm-cohomological-dimension-of-an-affine-curve] to each $C_j$ and
only its $q>1$ clause. By [lem-weil-pbc-finite-coproduct-cohomology-assembly], $H^q(C,\underline
M)=\prod_jH^q(C_j,\underline M)=0$ for $q>1$. The empty product is the zero abelian group.

3. For $q=1$, apply [sbc-lem-slicing] to the smooth composite $U\to S$ from the preceding step. It
gives a jointly surjective etale family $U_i\to U$, with $U_i\to V_i\to S$, both $U_i,V_i$ affine,
$U_i\to V_i$ smooth with geometrically connected fibres and a section. Because $S$ and $V_i$ are
affine, $V_i\to S$ is separated: its diagonal is induced by the surjection
$\Gamma(V_i)\otimes_A\Gamma(V_i)\to\Gamma(V_i)$. Apply [sbc-lem-etale-over-normal] to the integral
normal $S$, whose function field $L$ is separably closed. It writes $V_i$ as a disjoint union of
open-and-closed summands $V_{i\alpha}$, each identified with an open of $S$. First restrict $U_i$
to $U_{i\alpha}=U_i\times_{V_i}V_{i\alpha}$; this is open and closed in the affine $U_i$, hence
affine, and is still etale over $U$. Refine each nonempty $V_{i\alpha}$ by affine open subsets
$W$; this avoids assuming an arbitrary open of $S$ affine. Put
$C_W=U_{i\alpha}\times_{V_{i\alpha}}W$. This is affine, smooth with geometrically connected fibres
and a section over $W$. Since $W\to S$ is an open immersion, $C_W=U_{i\alpha}\times_SW$; the
relative dimension is one by the permanence node applied to the etale composite $U_{i\alpha}\to
U\to X$ and base change to $W$. The $C_W\to U$ still form an etale covering. Each nonempty affine
open $W$ is integral normal with function field $L$: its local rings are those of $S$, and its
generic point has the same local ring $L$. The canonical geometric generic point
$\operatorname{Spec}K\to S$ therefore factors through every such $W$. We may now work with one
pair $(C,W)$ of this form and the restriction $\xi\in H^1(C_K,\underline M)$.

4. The generic fibre $C_L$ is a smooth separated curve over $L$, and it is connected and nonempty
because $C\to W$ has geometrically connected fibres and a section. The map $C_K\to C_L$ is the
base change for the given field extension $L\subseteq K$ (here the specified generic factorization
of $g$ is essential). Apply [sbc-thm-algebraically-closed-invariance] in degree one; it supplies
$\bar\xi\in H^1(C_L,\underline M)$ pulling back to $\xi$. By [sbc-prop-torsors-h1], $\bar\xi$ is
represented by a finite etale $M$-torsor $P\to C_L$, naturally under pullback. Do not assume $P$
connected. For the next step fix the geometric base point $\sigma(\eta):\operatorname{Spec}L\to
C_L$ and a point $p$ in the fibre of $P$ there; the fibre is nonempty by the torsor condition and
is split by [lem-galois-geometric-fibre], since $L$ is algebraically closed.

5. Here is the connected-component argument in the unit, rather than the incorrect claim that
every torsor is connected. By [lem-fet-galois-category] and [thm-galois-abstract-equivalence],
finite etale covers of connected $C_L$ are finite continuous $\pi_1(C_L,\sigma(\eta))$-sets.
Identify the fibre of $P$ with $M$ using $p$. The monodromy is translation by the homomorphism
$f_P$ of [sbc-prop-torsors-h1]. Put $G=\operatorname{im}f_P\subset M$. The subset $G\subset M$ is
invariant and nonempty, so the equivalence realizes its inclusion as a finite etale subcover $Z\to
P$. The orbit decomposition $M=\coprod_{a\in M/G}(a+G)$ shows $Z$ is an open-and-closed component.
It is connected: a decomposition into two nonempty open-and-closed finite etale pieces would
decompose the transitive $\pi_1$-set $G$ into two invariant subsets. Translations by $G$ commute
with the monodromy and act simply transitively on its fibre; full faithfulness transports these
automorphisms and their group laws to $Z$. Thus $Z\to C_L$ is a finite etale Galois cover with
group $G$. The chosen label $0\in G$ is a point above $\sigma(\eta)$, rational over $L$ by [lem-
galois-geometric-fibre]. Its degree $|G|$ divides $n=|M|$ by the partition into cosets, hence is a
unit on $W$: if $n=|G|a$, an inverse is $a n^{-1}$. The map $Z\to P$ gives a section of
$Z\times_{C_L}P\to Z$, namely $z\mapsto(z,z\mapsto P)$; the torsor is therefore trivial on $Z$,
and the natural pointed bijection of [sbc-prop-torsors-h1] says $\bar\xi|_Z=0$.

6. Apply [sbc-thm-spreading-finite-etale-galois] with base $W$ and curve $C$. All its hypotheses
now hold: $W$ is affine, hence qcqs, integral normal; $C\to W$ is affine smooth, hence qcqs, has
geometrically connected fibres and the restricted section; $|G|$ is invertible; and $Z$ has the
required rational point over that section. Obtain a finite etale Galois cover $T\to C$ with
$T_L\simeq Z$. It is surjective by its torsor condition, so it is an etale covering of $C$.
Pulling $\bar\xi|_Z=0$ to $K$ gives $\xi|_{T_K}=0$. Explicitly, $T\times_CC_K\simeq
T_L\times_LK\simeq Z\times_LK$; functoriality of cohomology identifies restriction of $\xi$ with
pullback of $\bar\xi|_Z$. The torsor interpretation is natural by [sbc-prop-torsors-h1]. Composing
these covers with $C\to U$ from slicing kills the original class on an etale cover of $U$.

7. For completeness the local killing criterion needs no extra vanishing theorem. If every element
of an abelian presheaf $P$ is locally zero, any morphism $P\to F$ into a sheaf is zero: the image
of each element restricts to zero on a cover, so is zero by the uniqueness part of the sheaf
axiom. Apply this to the sheafification unit $P\to P^\#$ of [def-weil-pbc-sheafification]. The
unit is zero, and its universal property says there is a unique map $P^\#\to P^\#$ factoring it.
Both the identity and zero map do, so they agree, and $P^\#=0$. The preceding steps establish
local killing of $P_q$ first on affine objects and hence on all objects by their affine open
covers. Thus $R^qh_*\underline M=P_q^\#=0$ for every $q\geq1$.
-/
theorem isZero_universalCurveTest_higherDirectImage
    (A L K : Type u) [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [Field L] [Field K]
    [Algebra A L] [IsFractionRing A L] [IsAlgClosed L] [Algebra L K] [IsAlgClosed K]
    (hA : IsNormalScheme (Spec (.of A)))
    {X Y : Scheme.{u}} [IsAffine X] (f : X ⟶ Spec (.of A))
    [IsAffineHom f] [SmoothOfRelativeDimension 1 f]
    (h : Y ⟶ X) (e : Y ⟶ Spec (.of K))
    (sq : ChosenCartesianSquare h e f
      (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap L K)) ≫
        Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap A L))))
    (d : ℤ) (hd : IsUnit (d : A)) (q : ℤ) (hq : 1 ≤ q) :
    IsZero ((DerivedCategory.Plus.homologyFunctor
      (Sheaf X.smallEtaleTopology AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) q).obj
      ((Etale.rightDerivedLowerStarPlus AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1} h).obj
        ((DerivedCategory.Plus.singleFunctor
          (Sheaf Y.smallEtaleTopology AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) 0).obj
            (Y.constantZMod d.natAbs)))) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Etale
